-### Parameters
-*   **Error Tolerance $\epsilon$:** Controls the coverage of splats during generation [S1].
-*   **Frustum Parameters:** $near, top, bottom, h_{vp}$ are standard camera settings [S1].
-
-### Edge Cases and Pitfalls
-*   **Underdetermined Systems:** If a splat covers fewer than 2 samples, the normal fitting linear system is underdetermined [S1]. Use the pseudo-inverse to find the least-norm solution [S1].
-*   **Affine Approximation:** Do not use an affine approximation for projection [S1]. It disables the exact per-pixel parameterization required for correct clipping and depth, and while it allows EWA filtering, the object-space filter alone is sufficient [S1].
-*   **Backface Culling:** Optionally perform a conservative backface test in the vertex shader for closed models using the transformed normal [S1].
+### Optimization
+*   Render non-clipped splats first, then splats with one clipping line, then two clipping lines, to minimize shader switching overhead [S1].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-
-### Time and Memory Complexity
-*   **Memory:** 17 floats per splat (vs. 12 for standard elliptical splatting) [S1]. However, because Phong Splatting achieves comparable visual quality with ~10x fewer splats than flat shading, total memory usage is lower [S1].
-*   **Speed:** Achieves up to 4 million Phong-shaded, filtered, and blended splats per second on modern GPUs [S1].
-
-### Measured Performance
-*   **Quality vs. Count:** A torso model with 3,000 splats rendered with Phong Splatting achieves visual quality comparable to Phong-shaded triangle meshes [S1]. Flat shading requires 170,000 splats, and flat shading with Gaussian blending requires 33,000 splats for comparable quality [S1].
-
-### Strengths and Limitations
-*   **Strengths:** Projectively correct per-pixel parameterization; exact depth values prevent blending artifacts; superior shading quality compared to flat or Gouraud-like splatting; efficient GPU implementation using cube maps for lighting [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Cannot use EWA filtering (combined object/image space Gaussian) because it relies on exact projective mapping rather than affine approximation [S1]. Requires preprocessing to generate splats and fit normal fields [S1].
-
-### When to Use
-*   Use when high visual quality (smooth shading) is required for point-based surfaces without connectivity [S1].
-*   Use when rendering sharp features or clipped splats, as the method supports exact tangent-space clipping [S1].
-
-### When Not to Use
-*   Use [EWA Splatting](1069-ewa-splatting.md) if isotropic filtering and simpler kernel implementation are preferred over per-pixel shading quality [S1].
-*   Use [Deferred Surface Splatting](1072-deferred-surface-splatting.md) if deferred shading pipelines are already in place and per-pixel lighting overhead is a concern [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of splats. Each splat sends one vertex. Fragment processing is constant time per pixel within the splat footprint.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** 17 floats per splat. Higher per-primitive overhead than standard splatting (12 floats), but requires an order of magnitude fewer splats for comparable quality [S1].
+*   **Performance:** Achieves up to 4 million Phong shaded, filtered, and blended splats per second on modern GPUs [S1].
+*   **Quality:** Visual quality comparable to Phong shading in triangle meshes with ~3k splats, whereas flat shading requires ~170k splats and flat shading with Gaussian blending ~33k splats for comparable quality [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Projectively correct; exact per-pixel depth and normal computation; superior shading quality; efficient GPU implementation.
+*   **Limitations:** Does not support EWA splatting (combined object/image space filtering) because it avoids affine approximations; relies on object-space reconstruction filter alone, which is sufficient but not strictly band-limited [S1].
 
 ## Background
-Phong Splatting was introduced in [S1]. It builds on the concept of varying normal fields proposed by [KV01], but differs by using a dense input point cloud and a linear normal field fit over the splat area, rather than local Taylor expansions at the center [S1]. It addresses the limitations of earlier point-based rendering systems that used constant normals, resulting in flat shading artifacts [S1]. The method leverages the programmable pipeline of modern GPUs, specifically vertex and fragment shaders, to achieve real-time performance [S1].
+Phong Splatting was introduced by [S1] (Specht et al., 2004). It builds on the surface splatting framework and addresses the limitations of earlier methods like [KV01] (Kobbelt et al.), which used Taylor expansions for normal estimation and required clean datasets. Phong Splatting allows for robust rendering of technical datasets with sharp features using clipped splats and provides a projectively correct rendering pipeline. It compares favorably to deferred surface splatting [Sibling] by integrating lighting into the splat accumulation rather than deferring it, though deferred approaches may offer different flexibility in lighting models.
 
